:"people","H3559":"prepare","H1":"father","H4256":"division","H3791":"writing","H4385":"writing","H6391":"division","H251":"male-sibling","H2515":"division","H6942":"consecate","H1697":"word","H3027":"hand","H4872":"Moses","H7311":"to exalt","H6629":"flock","H3532":"lamb","H5795":"goat","H4672":"to find","H4557":"number","H7970":"thirty","H505":"thousand","H1241":"cattle","H7969":"three","H428":"these","H7399":"property","H8269":"ruler","H5071":"voluntariness","H2518":"Hilkiah","H2148":"Zechariah","H3171":"Jehiel","H5057":"leader","H8337":"six","H3967":"hundred","H3562":"Conaniah","H8098":"Shemaiah","H5417":"Nethanel","H2811":"Hashabiah","H3273":"Jeiel","H3107":"Jozacar","H2568":"five","H5977":"post","H4687":"commandment","H2236":"to scatter","H6584":"to strip","H5493":"remove","H5930":"burnt offering","H4653":"division","H7126":"come","H3789":"to write","H5612":"document","H3651":"right","H1310":"to boil","H784":"fire","H4941":"judgement","H5518":"pot","H1731":"pot","H6745":"pot","H7323":"run","H310":"after","H3588":"for","H175":"Aaron","H5927":"rise","H2459":"fat","H5704":"till","H3915":"night","H7891":"to sing","H623":"Asaph","H4612":"office","H1968":"Heman","H3038":"Jeduthun","H2374":"seer","H7778":"gatekeeper","H8179":"gate","H3117":"day","H1931":"he/she/it","H4196":"altar","H6256":"time","H2282":"feast","H4682":"unleavened bread","H7651":"seven","H3808":"not","H3644":"like","H8050":"Samuel","H5030":"prophet","H3063":"Judah","H3427":"to dwell","H8083":"eight","H8141":"year","H4438":"royalty","H2088":"this","H2063":"this","H5224":"Neco","H4714":"Egypt","H3898":"to fight","H3751":"Carchemish","H6578":"Euphrates","H3318":"come","H7125":"befall","H7971":"depart","H413":"to(wards)","H4397":"messenger","H4100":"what?","H859":"you(m.s.)","H4421":"battle","H926":"to dismay","H2308":"to cease","H5973":"with","H408":"not","H7843":"to ruin","H5437":"turn","H6440":"before","H4480":"from","H2664":"to search","H8085":"hear","H6310":"lip","H935":"come","H1237":"Valley (of Jericho)","H4023":"Megiddo","H3384":"to shoot","H5650":"servant/slave","H5674":"to pass","H2470":"weak","H3966":"much","H4818":"chariot","H7392":"to ride","H7393":"chariot","H4932":"Second [Quarter]","H3212":"to walk","H4191":"to die","H6912":"to bury","H6913":"grave","H56":"to mourn","H6969":"to chant","H3414":"Jeremiah","H7015":"dirge","H2706":"decree","H2005":"look!","H3499":"remainder","H2617":"kindness","H8451":"instruction","H314":"last"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",3,null,"haydock","By whose. Septuagint, “the powerful in Israel, that they might be sanctified to the Lord; and they put,” &c. Hebrew, “who instructed all Israel, the holy people of the Lord: Put,” &c. (Haydock) — No more. Hebrew adds, “upon your shoulders;” (Haydock) whence it is inferred, that it had been carried about the cities of Juda, under the impious kings, who would not suffer it in the temple. (Calmet) — Some believe that it was kept in the house of Sellum, the uncle of Jeremias, and husband of Olda. (St. Jerome, Trad.) (Tirinus)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Families. Hebrew adds, “according to the divisions of the families of the fathers of your brethren, the people, (Haydock; or laics. Calmet) and the division of the families of the Levites.” All were placed, with great order, in the temple."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Which. Hebrew, “of the Lord, by the hand of Moses,” which ordinances he has written down. (Menochius)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","In. Hebrew, “for the Passover-offerings, from the flock, lambs and kids, in number 30,000.” Et reliqui pecoris, is not expressed. (Haydock) — The small cattle might be intended for other sacrifices. (Menochius)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Offered. Hebrew, “gave to the people.” (Haydock) — Rulers: the first alone was high priest. (Calmet) — Small cattle: literally, cattle of different sorts;” commixtim. Protestants supply, “small cattle,” which is not now in Hebrew. (Haydock) — Syriac and Arabic have, “sheep;” but we had best follow (Calmet) the Septuagint, “sheep, both lambs and kids.” Either would suffice, Exodus xii. 5."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Cattle. Protestants again supply these words. Septuagint have, “sheep.” (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Blood is expressed in the Septuagint and understood in Hebrew. (Haydock) — With. Hebrew and Septuagint, “from their hands;” (Haydock) receiving it from the officers, who might slay their own victims, (Calmet) unless they were unclean, chap. xxx. 17. (Haydock) — Holocausts. Hebrew and Septuagint speak of the paschal lambs. (Haydock) — Holocausts were regularly to be flayed by priests, Leviticus i. 6. The hurry of the solemnity, (Calmet) and necessity, here excused the Levites, (Menochius) if we understand proper holocausts. (Haydock)"],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Separated. Septuagint, “prepared the holocaust to give; having taken notice to whom the victims belonged, before they took off the skin, (Menochius) or rather, separating these from the other peace-offerings. (Tournemine)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Victims. Hebrew and Septuagint, “but the holy offerings they boiled.” See Exodus xii. 9. (Haydock)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Prophets. Hebrew, “seer.” But Septuagint, &c., read in the plural. These were “prophets of the king,” (Haydock) or masters of music. (Calmet) — Vulgate might insinuate that these three gave orders, as well as David. But they were in office under him, as people of the same name were, probably, under Josias. (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","As Josias kept. He displayed greater liberality (Estius. See 4 Kings xxiii. 20.) and devotion; and the festival had also been neglected for some time. (Worthington)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Charcamis belonged to the Assyrian monarch, (Isaias x. 9.) who subdued the empire of Babylon, chap. xxxiii. 11."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","With me. Grotius thinks that Jeremias had given the order; and the author of Hebrew Traditions, supposes that he forbade Josias to oppose Pharao, who, it seems, was assured of victory. (Calmet) — But this is uncertain; and Josias might justly suspect that the king of Egypt spoke without authority. (Haydock) — The augurs of the latter might declare the truth, (Menochius) though they could not dive into futurity. (Haydock) — Herodotus (i.) informs us, that Nechos fought against the Syrians, (or Israelites) in Magdelum, and took Cadythis, a city as large as Sardis, of Lydia; probably Jerusalem, (Du Hamel) or Cades. (Calmet)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Prepared. Hebrew, “disguised himself,” like Achab; (Calmet) or set his army in array. Septuagint, “he was bent on fighting him.” (Haydock) — He supposed that Pharao intended to invade his dominions, and God would withdraw him from the world. (Worthington)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","After the manner of kings, is not in Hebrew, &c. (Haydock) — Curtius (iv.) informs us, that the Persian monarch had always a horse behind his chariot, to be ready in case of any accident."],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Lamentations. Some think that we have them in the Bible. (Josephus, &c.) — Others believe that they are lost. (Salien, the year of the world 3425.) — Law, on the anniversary; (Menochius) or when any calamity occurs, the lamentations of Jeremias are used. (Tirinus) — There were collections of such poems, as well as of canticles, for victory and marriages. (Calmet)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Israel is placed first in Hebrew and Septuagint. (Haydock) — The kings of Juda assumed the title, as the kingdom had been subverted above a century; and the wretched remains of Israel had retreated into the territories, (Calmet) or acknowledged their dominion. (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}